{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EWGVb2PfJHxozz3HEFRpQG","type":"tool_result","content":"420\tThe 3-LF framework, while sufficient for current purposes, may benefit from additional LFs targeting semantic stagnation patterns that structural features handle less well (outcome-only subtype, F1$_\\text{stag}$ = 0.72).\n421\t\n422\t\\section{Conclusion}\n423\t\n424\tWe present a systematic empirical study demonstrating that progress stagnation in LLM agents is detectable through simple structural features, without requiring semantic understanding, expensive embeddings, or LLM inference.\n425\tOn 650 gold-annotated windows, structural features (0.89 macro-F1) surpass neural embedding probes ($-$8\\,pp) and LLM-as-judge ($-$22\\,pp) at negligible cost.\n426\tWe quantify the circular evaluation artifact inherent in weak supervision ($\\sim$10.5\\,pp) and validate temporal onset dynamics at 80\\% gold alignment.\n427\tThe failure of three temporal label model implementations to improve over majority vote provides an important negative result constraining future research.\n428\tOur work redirects the agent monitoring conversation: before investing in representation learning or LLM-based judges, practitioners should first establish whether simple structural features suffice---in the case of stagnation detection, they do.\n429\t\n430\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n431\t\n432\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Yang et al.}2024]{yang2024swe}\n433\tJohn Yang, Carlos~E. Jimenez, Alexander Wettig, Kilian Lieret, Shunyu Yao, Karthik Narasimhan, and Ofir Press.\n434\t\\newblock 2024.\n435\t\\newblock {SWE}-agent: Agent-computer interfaces enable automated software engineering.\n436\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n437\t\n438\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Xie et al.}2026]{xie2026agentprm}\n439\tZhiheng Xie et al.\n440\t\\newblock 2026.\n441\t\\newblock {AgentPRM}: Process reward models for {LLM} agent evaluation.\n442\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACM Web Conference}.\n443\t\n444\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Chen et al.}2025]{iclr2025monitoring}\n445\tAnonymous.\n446\t\\newblock 2025.\n447\t\\newblock Monitoring {LLM} agents for sequentially contextual harm.\n448\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2025 Workshop}.\n449\t\n450\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zhang et al.}2026]{slopcodebench2026}\n451\tAnonymous.\n452\t\\newblock 2026.\n453\t\\newblock {SlopCodeBench}: Quantifying coding agent degradation in long-horizon tasks.\n454\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2603.24755}.\n455\t\n456\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Ratner et al.}2016]{ratner2016data}\n457\tAlexander Ratner, Christopher De~Sa, Sen Wu, Daniel Selsam, and Christopher R{\\'e}.\n458\t\\newblock 2016.\n459\t\\newblock Data programming: Creating large training sets, quickly.\n460\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n461\t\n462\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2025]{weak2strong2025}\n463\tAnonymous.\n464\t\\newblock 2025.\n465\t\\newblock Reliable weak-to-strong monitoring of {LLM} agents.\n466\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2508.19461}.\n467\t\n468\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Shinn et al.}2023]{shinn2023reflexion}\n469\tNoah Shinn, Federico Cassano, Ashwin Gopinath, Karthik~R Narasimhan, and Shunyu Yao.\n470\t\\newblock 2023.\n471\t\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n472\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n473\t\n474\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zhou et al.}2024]{zhou2024lats}\n475\tAndy Zhou, Kai Yan, Michal Shlapentokh-Rothman, Haohan Wang, and Yu-Xiong Wang.\n476\t\\newblock 2024.\n477\t\\newblock Language agent tree search unifies reasoning, acting, and planning in language models.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICML}.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Yao et al.}2024]{yao2024tree}\n481\tShunyu Yao, Dian Yu, Jeffrey Zhao, Izhak Shafran, Thomas~L Griffiths, Yuan Cao, and Karthik Narasimhan.\n482\t\\newblock 2024.\n483\t\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n484\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n485\t\n486\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2025]{prm_framework2025}\n487\tAnonymous.\n488\t\\newblock 2025.\n489\t\\newblock A process reward model framework for multi-step reasoning.\n490\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.10325}.\n491\t\n492\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2026]{toolprmbench2026}\n493\tAnonymous.\n494\t\\newblock 2026.\n495\t\\newblock {ToolPRMBench}: Benchmarking process reward models for tool use.\n496\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.12294}.\n497\t\n498\t\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2026]{traj_reward2026}\n499\tAnonymous.\n500\t\\newblock 2026.\n501\t\\newblock Trajectory-level reward modeling for {LLM} agents.\n502\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2604.08178}.\n503\t\n504\t\\end{thebibliography}\n505\t\n506\t\\end{document}\n507\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd3eeb9-72a8-41c0-a362-eb2d8ee675d3","uuid":"f8d499b6-aace-413a-87d4-36ac4dbe43c9","timestamp":"2026-05-14T19:01:56.080Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T184058/best/paper.tex","content":"The 3-LF framework, while sufficient for current purposes, may benefit from additional LFs targeting semantic stagnation patterns that structural features handle less well (outcome-only subtype, F1$_\\text{stag}$ = 0.72).\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present a systematic empirical study demonstrating that progress stagnation in LLM agents is detectable through simple structural features, without requiring semantic understanding, expensive embeddings, or LLM inference.\nOn 650 gold-annotated windows, structural features (0.89 macro-F1) surpass neural embedding probes ($-$8\\,pp) and LLM-as-judge ($-$22\\,pp) at negligible cost.\nWe quantify the circular evaluation artifact inherent in weak supervision ($\\sim$10.5\\,pp) and validate temporal onset dynamics at 80\\% gold alignment.\nThe failure of three temporal label model implementations to improve over majority vote provides an important negative result constraining future research.\nOur work redirects the agent monitoring conversation: before investing in representation learning or LLM-based judges, practitioners should first establish whether simple structural features suffice---in the case of stagnation detection, they do.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Yang et al.}2024]{yang2024swe}\nJohn Yang, Carlos~E. Jimenez, Alexander Wettig, Kilian Lieret, Shunyu Yao, Karthik Narasimhan, and Ofir Press.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\\newblock {SWE}-agent: Agent-computer interfaces enable automated software engineering.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Xie et al.}2026]{xie2026agentprm}\nZhiheng Xie et al.\n\\newblock 2026.\n\\newblock {AgentPRM}: Process reward models for {LLM} agent evaluation.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACM Web Conference}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Chen et al.}2025]{iclr2025monitoring}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2025.\n\\newblock Monitoring {LLM} agents for sequentially contextual harm.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2025 Workshop}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zhang et al.}2026]{slopcodebench2026}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2026.\n\\newblock {SlopCodeBench}: Quantifying coding agent degradation in long-horizon tasks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2603.24755}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Ratner et al.}2016]{ratner2016data}\nAlexander Ratner, Christopher De~Sa, Sen Wu, Daniel Selsam, and Christopher R{\\'e}.\n\\newblock 2016.\n\\newblock Data programming: Creating large training sets, quickly.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2025]{weak2strong2025}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2025.\n\\newblock Reliable weak-to-strong monitoring of {LLM} agents.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2508.19461}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Shinn et al.}2023]{shinn2023reflexion}\nNoah Shinn, Federico Cassano, Ashwin Gopinath, Karthik~R Narasimhan, and Shunyu Yao.\n\\newblock 2023.\n\\newblock Reflexion: Language agents with verbal reinforcement learning.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Zhou et al.}2024]{zhou2024lats}\nAndy Zhou, Kai Yan, Michal Shlapentokh-Rothman, Haohan Wang, and Yu-Xiong Wang.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\\newblock Language agent tree search unifies reasoning, acting, and planning in language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Yao et al.}2024]{yao2024tree}\nShunyu Yao, Dian Yu, Jeffrey Zhao, Izhak Shafran, Thomas~L Griffiths, Yuan Cao, and Karthik Narasimhan.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\\newblock Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2025]{prm_framework2025}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2025.\n\\newblock A process reward model framework for multi-step reasoning.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.10325}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2026]{toolprmbench2026}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2026.\n\\newblock {ToolPRMBench}: Benchmarking process reward models for tool use.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.12294}.\n\n\\bibitem[\\protect\\citename{Anonymous}2026]{traj_reward2026}\nAnonymous.\n\\newblock 2026.\n\\newblock Trajectory-level reward modeling for {LLM} agents.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2604.08178}.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":88,"startLine":420,"totalLines":507}}}
